// @run
// @log <fld>
// @hint redundant type annotation: `describe` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `b` is inferred as `{r: f64, toString: () => string}` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `: string` / `: boolean` return annotations below are DECLARED rather than
// deleted: a fixture for a type feature should read as the documentation it is,
// and the checker calling them redundant is a fact about inference, not about
// the example. (`@hint` is how a corpus file says "yes, on purpose".)
// THE SECOND WITNESS: a zero-ary closure FIELD satisfies a method bound.
//
// This is not a special case in the implementation and the fixture exists to keep
// it from becoming one. A method member `{ toString(): string }` is stored as the
// plain function type `() => string` — the call's arguments, no receiver slot — so
// a field of that exact type satisfies it by ordinary assignability. The
// directionality falls out of the same fact: every field satisfies the method
// bound, and a UFCS-satisfied value does NOT satisfy a field bound, because it
// carries no closure.
//
// Measured 2026-09-01, and the reason the design chose the parens spelling over
// `(self)`: a field of the UFCS function's own type (`(Circle) => string`) does
// NOT make `x.toString()` work — that call is spelled `x.toString(x)`. The true
// twin of a UFCS `self`-function on the CALL is the zero-ary field, which is what
// this bound demands and what the value below carries.
type Showable = { toString(): string }
type Boxed = { r: f64, toString: () => string }

function describe<T: Showable>(x: T): string { "<" + x.toString() + ">" }

const b: Boxed = { r: 1.0, toString: () => "fld" }
print(describe(b))
